This dissertation investigates UAV-based monitoring of cotton maturation during the harvest-aid stage in Xinjiang, using defoliation rate and boll opening rate as the two agronomic indicators. It addresses complex field backgrounds, changing illumination, inefficient manual sampling, and edge-deployment constraints through dataset construction, model design, low-light adaptation, and field workflow validation. Cotton maturation status is represented as a computable remote-sensing phenotype.

First, a dataset of cotton defoliation rate and boll opening rate covering multiple acquisition periods, diverse illumination conditions, and complex field backgrounds is established. The study further standardizes image acquisition, manual ground-truth statistics, position parsing, sample organization, and evaluation protocols, providing a consistent data foundation for subsequent experiments. Second, vectorized RGB pixel values are used to establish engineering baselines with PCA plus logistic regression and random-forest variants, and Accuracy and Macro-F1 are used to analyze dual-indicator recognition under class imbalance. On this basis, a structured-feature scheme involving spectral indices, foreground segmentation, morphology, and texture is analyzed as a direction for subsequent interpretable modeling and experimental validation.

To satisfy edge-deployment constraints, a lightweight dual-indicator recognition model, RTCMNet (Real-Time Cotton Monitoring Network), is proposed. The model integrates a shared backbone, multi-scale convolutional attention, and dual classification heads, achieving a five-fold cross-validation mean accuracy of $0.93 plus.minus 0.02$ across the two tasks with only 0.37 M parameters and 0.19 GMACs, while requiring 32 ms per image on an edge device, thereby enabling real-time joint recognition of defoliation rate and boll opening rate. To further address low-light and complex illumination scenarios, the dissertation develops a paired low-light data generation pipeline inspired by physical imaging and ISP mechanisms. In the RGB domain, the pipeline jointly simulates exposure attenuation, white-balance drift, Poisson-Gaussian noise, mild blur, and vignetting, and uses real nighttime UAV images for statistical calibration, thus providing controllable supervision for low-light enhancement and task-applicability evaluation.

A lightweight enhancement front-end, $L^3$-AgriUAVNet, is then cascaded with RTCMNet. Using the HVI color space and an RFDN-S backbone, the model has 0.139 M parameters and 6.252 GMACs. It achieves the highest SSIM (0.893) among the compared methods, a PSNR of 26.24 dB, and an inference time of 202 ms per image (4.95 FPS) on an iPad-class device. On real sunset cotton-field imagery, predictions before and after enhancement show task- and stage-dependent distribution changes, confirming the feasibility of cascaded inference. As synchronous manual labels were not collected, this experiment analyzes prediction distributions without quantifying accuracy gains. In a maize-seedling task with detection ground truth, enhanced inputs increase YOLOv8n mAP\@0.5 by 14.6% (from 0.1393 to 0.1596), whereas YOLOv8s performs better with Raw input. Enhancement should therefore be configured according to detector capacity and input distribution. Multi-temporal UAV acquisition, recognition, geographic mapping, and field observations are finally combined to analyze the spatial and temporal patterns of cotton maturation.

The study provides a reference for intelligent cotton maturation monitoring, harvest-aid decision support, and agricultural visual perception under changing illumination.
